ank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T18:03:33-08:00","id":1000060,"title":"","content":"I am most impressed with this, and no doubt will make use of it in the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T20:28:20-08:00","id":1000115,"title":"","content":"Cool! What system are you using for all of this? The old one?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T20:42:15-08:00","id":1000119,"title":"","content":"This is made for the <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/scionoverhaul/home\" target=\"_blank\">Scion 2.0 system</a>, an increasingly modified version of First Edition. We will adding the Kami onto the website 'soon', the moment I have some free time. There will be a few extra things, such as a family tree for the Pantheon, and some little extra commentary put in in sidebars here and there. If you want to check out the other Pantheon revisions we have done, the <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/scionoverhaul/pantheons/tuatha-de-danann\" target=\"_blank\">Tuatha</a> and the <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/scionoverhaul/pantheons/theoi\" target=\"_blank\">Theoi</a> have both been completed.<br /> <br /> Despite using our own system hack, all of this can be altered to work pretty easily within RAW or whatever 1E houseruled system you desire really. Biggest difference is that we divided up the Sky Purview into Sky and Storms. If you want to use a system without this division, just exchange all instances of Storms for Sky, and it would all be fixed up!<br /> <br /> We also do not present the Titans we write up as pure antagonists, we use a concept of Titan similar to Gotham By Night when they were still up and running. Colossal entities caught up in their concept, and relatively withdrawn from the world due to that. The games we have run tend to run less along the lines of RAW's Titan War, and more in Pantheon-Specific ideas. So, for example, the Irish are normally primarily focused on grumbling about losing their land to mortals, and plotting to take it back. The idea I like most for the Kami is internal strife, as the Amatsukami are very Imperial, subjugating another branch of the Kami, leaving a Civil War plot hook rather interesting in my mind.<br /> <br /> If anyone has any questions, feel free to just toss them up here. We are more than happy to explain, or discuss whatever we can.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T21:00:23-08:00","id":1000124,"title":"","content":"Color me impressed, I also really enjoyed your take on the Theoi and the Tuatha De Danann.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11643-corax","name":"Corax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T21:00:40-08:00","id":1000125,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=999871#post999871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Suicide</b><br /> Japan has a noteworthy relationship with suicide, having the seventeenth highest suicide rate by the World Health Organization. Though suicide does not appear in the higher cosmological myths of the Kami, the understanding and role of suicide is so divergently different from many other parts of the world. It is believed that suicide in Japan has roots in social obligation more so than mental illness as it is in the West, with these social obligations finding their genesis in both Buddhist and Samurai practice influencing popular culture. Buddhism brought to Japan self-immolation, a suicide which could benefit the karma of one\u2019s community, self-mummification practiced by specific sects to achieve enlightenment, and self-abandonment, where one goes adrift at sea for rescue by Kannon. The Samurai on the other hand had a more famed and ritualised version of self-disembowelment, and though this practice (named Seppuku) is ancient, it was initially uncommon.<br /> <br /> During the Middle Ages, rife with war, suicide amongst the Samurai became more common, though not ritualised, instead quick battlefield suicides were employed to avoid capture. As time moved on, this chaotic period ended, but suicide remained a part of the culture to the point that the Tokugawa shogunate classified Seppuku as a honorable form of execution of Samurai. This is the point where Seppuku takes a form more recognised in modernity, ritualised self-disembowelment, though the exact details of this complex practice will not be detailed here, only partly for concerns of length. The reasons for these suicides however is the important part, and fall into two large categories, willingly preformed ones, and forced ones.<br /> <br /> In addition to defeat in battle (to avoid dishonorable treatment by an enemy force), voluntary reasons included self-punishment (on account of a personal failure), to protect one\u2019s family (collective punishments for criminals was the norm, though the Samurai were able to save one\u2019s family if they were to commit seppuku), to shame one\u2019s lord, as proof of one\u2019s innocence (or, more aptly, in indignation of a false accusation), or to follow one\u2019s lord into death. Situations in which this could be forced upon a Samurai is both by an enemy (with the famed case of Oda Nobunaga trapped in a burning building with his loyal retainers), or as legal punishment.<br /> <br /> While these notions may be seen as relics of the past, they survive into the modern era, though without the intense ritualisation. Salarymen burning out or cracking under insane pressure are often driven to it, which likely leaves the Kami with a somewhat unique outlook on suicide. To ancient Japan, suicide was an action of bravery, which may lead to a large degree of confusion with other Pantheons.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Just want to point out that if a samurai died, it was socially expected for his wife to commit suicide.<br /> <br /> Being the wife of a samurai must have been pretty terrifying... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T22:11:01-08:00","id":1000147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000125#post1000125\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Just want to point out that if a samurai died, it was socially expected for his wife to commit suicide.<br /> <br /> Being the wife of a samurai must have been pretty terrifying... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Hm, where have you heard this? I haven't encountered such a norm in my research - in fact, I instead encountered remarried widows, and the concept of samurai committing suicide to force their lord to provide for his widow and kids, as was his duty. Could you point me to your source so I can check?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T22:29:25-08:00","id":1000151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sacerdos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000147#post1000147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hm, where have you heard this? I haven't encountered such a norm in my research - in fact, I instead encountered remarried widows, and the concept of samurai committing suicide to force their lord to provide for his widow and kids, as was his duty. Could you point me to your source so I can check?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <b>Number 6 on this video.</b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CDx0RoE3zZ8\" data-code=\"CDx0RoE3zZ8\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/CDx0RoE3zZ8/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-07T23:21:11-08:00","id":1000164,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000151#post1000151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <b>Number 6 on this video.</b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CDx0RoE3zZ8\" data-code=\"CDx0RoE3zZ8\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/CDx0RoE3zZ8/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>So, you probably noticed that the video in general is a bit exaggerated, and doesn't cite any sources itself, but they make an important distinction - the wives aren't expected to suicide when their husbands died, as you put it, but are expected to commit suicide after their husbands already committed ritual suicide. And that puts us in a whole new realm of complexity. The video generalises about a millennium of historical development on samurai practices into 7 minutes - and the same goes for our short culture entries here. Both present only the gist of an issue, and, in case of the video, the most &quot;horrifying&quot; elements of it. As you can tell from our culture entries, all these practices changed and developed over a long time (if you want more detail on any specific era or stage of development, maybe rather PM me before I start dragging this whole thread into a drawn-out discussion).<br /> <br /> Generalising again, I can say that a wife committing suicide alongside her husband was not common practice - as I've mentioned, committing honourable suicide was actually a way for a retainer to force his lord into providing for his family and to exempt them from collective punishment. But, of course, there was never a standard case of <i>seppuku</i>, as there was never a standard case of any cultural practice. As we've explained, <i>seppuku</i> was often forced and used as punishment, and in such cases, family, friends, or servants of the condemned could be forced to join in on the belly-slitting, and yes, in such case a wife could be affected, too. But it was in no way the usual procedure.<br /> <br /> Such collective punishment of the (innocent) family members of a criminal alongside themselves was very common in all of East Asia, and was supposed to act as a strong deterrent against crime, although it was of course mainly used in political cases. Before the beginning of the age of samurai, Japan was actually quite queasy about capital punishment in general, but collective punishment was carried out widely in China, and it is, sadly, used in North Korea to this day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T10:50:42-08:00","id":1000328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=999877#post999877\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Aesir</b>: The Aesir and the Kami have had the most limited of contact, with the only interaction coming from the wandering All-Father, who it really must be said does not make a good first impression with a Pantheon run by a Goddess. If the two Pantheons were to find themselves interacting more closely, the Pantheon\u2019s traditions of hospitality and politics vary so wildly the relationship could be nothing but exceptionally strained.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not contradicting, but I would like some clarification on why Odin would have a hard time making a good impression on a pantheon run by a goddess, especially considering she came from shamanistic roots, he came from shamanistic roots, and he's spent time as a woman to learn more of those secrets. <br /> <br /> I mean, certainly it seems to me that he'd have far less difficulty making a good impression with her than Zeus, who would probably spend all his time ignoring the woman and exchanging stories with Susano-O, or Ra, who is absolutely convinced that Amateratsu must be filling a role similar to Isis, and be looking for the 'actual' Pharaoh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T11:09:16-08:00","id":1000346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000328#post1000328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not contradicting, but I would like some clarification on why Odin would have a hard time making a good impression on a pantheon run by a goddess, especially considering she came from shamanistic roots, he came from shamanistic roots, and he's spent time as a woman to learn more of those secrets.<br /> <br /> I mean, certainly it seems to me that he'd have far less difficulty making a good impression with her than Zeus, who would probably spend all his time ignoring the woman and exchanging stories with Susano-O, or Ra, who is absolutely convinced that Amateratsu must be filling a role similar to Isis, and be looking for the 'actual' Pharaoh.</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's see, how would I phrase this. Odin is a bit of a dick, so, right off the bat he isn't making a good first impression. Now, I am not exceptionally well versed in Norse myth, I am not sure in which Edda Odin becomes a woman, (I'm expecting it's one of the accusations when Loki bardges in and they spend ages calling each other names?) but I do know that the Norse and the Japanese had very different conceptions of femininity. As gender (and sex can be argued as well to be this) is a social construct, Odin having been a woman for a time does not nessessarily mean he would have any real points of similarity or conection with a Feudal Japanese woman.<br /> <br /> Additionally, while both have Shamanistic links, Shamanism is not an overarching, pan-cultural system. Norse shamanism and Japanese shamanism are not the same, despite us just slapping similar names on them. So, from that angle, points of connection also break down. Add to this Amaterasu would be more than happy to tell Odin he was doing it <i>wrong</i>, since the Kami are incredibly ethnocentric, that would sour the relationship even more since Odin would take that like a brick to the skull.<br /> <br /> Continuing, while Odin may have spent time as a women, he doesn't nessessarily respect women. Women in positions of absolute unquestioned authority would be a bit of a cultural deviation from Odin's perspective, and he is not always the most polite, which would also be a source of overwhelming offense with the Kami.<br /> <br /> Essentially, while there may be conceptual similarities, Odin and Amaterasu have entirely different experiences and expectations of the other. While Odin may have been a woman for a time, a Norse Woman is not a Japanese Woman. Further, while Odin and Amaterasu may have shamanistic links, a Japanese Shaman and a Norse Shaman are not the same thing at all due to wildly different cosmological structures. Odin will expect a very different form of hospitality than he would recieve, and Odin's responce to perceived poor hospitality would tank social relationships.<br /> <br /> Further, in our games, Odin is normally a bit... how to put it. He wants to avoid Ragnarok, and as part of a raiding culture, he's utterly content taking other people, or Pantheon's, stuff.<br /> <br /> Oh, also, Odin is all about Death. He has carrion birds as his servants. Odin is a giant, radiating source of corruption, and he might actually be flat out banned from entering the Japanese Overworld due to that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T11:31:35-08:00","id":1000359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000346#post1000346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Odin is a bit of a dick, so, right off the bat he isn't making a good first impression.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's a dick to his brother, there's no real evidence aside from the way he treats Loki that he is a huge dick on the scale that the other gods in various pantheons are. He's also the consummate diplomat. While Tyr could be said to represent the Eddard Stark approach to divine kingship, he ended up losing a hand for it, and many scholars argue that this was symbolic of Odin's rise to prominence among Norse peoples, because Odin is more than honorable, he's pragmatic. He has to remain on good terms with the Vanir, and arguably the Alfar (or Frey wouldn't have a hall in Ljossalfheim, playing at ambassador). Even his dickery with the Wall of Asgard could be explained as an attempt to save face with the other gods. His mother is a jotun, Thor's mother is a jotun, Skadi is a jotun who lives in Asgard, Odin's clearly not above being diplomatic with his enemy either. Also, Loki is his blood brother, so that's significant as well. Things only <i>really</i> start to escalate between them when Odin has a vision that Loki's children with Angrboda will be a threat to the Aesir, and abducts them. Loki responds by killing Baldur, and around and around we go.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, I am not exceptionally well versed in Norse myth, I am not sure in which Edda Odin becomes a woman, (I'm expecting it's one of the accusations when Loki bardges in and they spend ages calling each other names?) </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;The other main form of Germanic shamanism is contained within the magical tradition known as </span></span></span><a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/concepts/seidr/\" target=\"_blank\">seidr</a><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">, of which Odin and Freya are the foremost divine practitioners. In traditional Germanic society, for a man to engage in seidr was effectively to forsake the male gender role, which brought considerable scorn upon any male who chose to take up this path. As the sagas show, this didn\u2019t stop some men from practicing seidr anyway. However, even Odin wasn\u2019t exempt from such charges of \u201cunmanliness,\u201d and was taunted for adopting the feminine traits and tasks that form part of the backbone of seidr. Saxo, in the passage on Odin\u2019s exile alluded to above, relates that \u201cby his stage-tricks and his assumption of a woman\u2019s work he had brought the foulest scandal on the name of the gods.\u201d</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">[16]</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"> Note also the reference to being \u201cfertilized\u201d in the verse quoted above \u2013 while this is certainly a metaphor, it\u2019s a metaphor loaded with sexual implications that would have been immediately recognizable to any Viking Age or medieval reader or hearer of the poem. A fuller discussion of the relationship between Germanic shamanism and gender roles can be found </span></span></span><a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/concepts/shamanism/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">. For our present purposes, it\u2019s sufficient to point out that, in the eyes of the pre-Christian northern Europeans, Odin\u2019s practice of seidr made him a rather \u201cunmanly\u201d being incapable of fulfilling the expectations placed upon an honorable man.&quot;</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/gods-and-creatures/the-aesir-gods-and-goddesses/odin/\" target=\"_blank\">http://norse-mythology.org/gods-and-...oddesses/odin/</a> is where the quote is from, it's a fairly decent site.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> but I do know that the Norse and the Japanese had very different conceptions of femininity. As gender (and sex can be argued as well to be this) is a social construct, Odin having been a woman for a time does not nessessarily mean he would have any real points of similarity or conection with a Feudal Japanese woman. </div> </div> </div>Okay, but at this point you're arguing that any woman would have the same problem making a first impression with a pantheon lead by a woman, because Our Women Are Different\u2122. Even the Tuatha would lose their 'edge' because their notion of what a female leader is like would differ from what Japan believes a woman should be - to say nothing of the fact that you already expressed Amateratsu being out of sorts about Japan's stance on women. Why wouldn't she respect a culture where a man viewed her magic and shamanistic history as valuable enough to sacrifice his dignity and honor for? Why wouldn't she respect a culture where one of the chief war gods is a woman, and whose people actively pray to a Disir (Female Ancestor) to guide and protect them in battle?<br /> <br /> Again, it's not that I have an issue with the Kami and the Aesir not getting along, it's that you specifically used the example that they only really see Odin, and Odin has a problem with women leading.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Additionally, while both have Shamanistic links, Shamanism is not an overarching, pan-cultural system. Norse shamanism and Japanese shamanism are not the same, despite us just slapping similar names on them. So, from that angle, points of connection also break down. Add to this Amaterasu would be more than happy to tell Odin he was doing it <i>wrong</i>, since the Kami are incredibly ethnocentric, that would sour the relationship even more since Odin would take that like a brick to the skull. </div> </div> </div>We call both of them the same thing because they enjoy many similar facets. The Norse have Land Wights, the Japanese had spirits of the land worth placating and working with, just as norse shamans worked with their own indigenous spirits. There are going to be technical differences, because that's true of anything sharing a similar label across significant geographical difference, but we wouldn't assign them the same name if they weren't comparable ways of thinking.<br /> <br /> Also Odin would have absolutely no issue being taught a different way of doing things. He goes out of his way to learn the magic of every race he can, and again, sacrificed his dignity and honor in order to be 'put in his place' by a woman.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Continuing, while Odin may have spent time as a women, he doesn't nessessarily respect women. Women in positions of absolute unquestioned authority would be a bit of a cultural deviation from Odin's perspective, and he is not always the most polite, which would also be a source of overwhelming offense with the Kami. </div> </div> </div>Again, plenty of people would argue that Odin is incredibly sympathetic to women, his most trusted servants, the valkyries, are women. Freya is their leader and arguably the leader of her group in Asgard because Njord is a bit of a pushover, honestly. The Jotuns are a matriarchal people (Angrboda leads those in the Ironwood, Loki takes the name of his mother 'Laufeysson'), so we also can't claim that he's never been exposed to women in power, to say nothing of his inability to topple Hela, who is queen of the 'other' afterlife.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Essentially, while there may be conceptual similarities, Odin and Amaterasu have entirely different experiences and expectations of the other. While Odin may have been a woman for a time, a Norse Woman is not a Japanese Woman. Further, while Odin and Amaterasu may have shamanistic links, a Japanese Shaman and a Norse Shaman are not the same thing at all due to wildly different cosmological structures. Odin will expect a very different form of hospitality than he would recieve, and Odin's responce to perceived poor hospitality would tank social relationships. </div> </div> </div>The Norse pantheons are some of the most 'racially diverse' in the world, and Odin is a diplomat to basically every race in it, if any god can handle different customs of hospitality, it would be him.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh, also, Odin is all about Death. He has carrion birds as his servants. Odin is a giant, radiating source of corruption, and he might actually be flat out banned from entering the Japanese Overworld due to that. </div> </div> </div>The ravens are symbols of higher consciousness and reality, representing thought and memory. They are carrion birds simply because carrion birds will gravitate to a battlefield, therefor they must be Odin's messengers. If anything the Kami would empathize with his constant struggle for dominion over death with the far more corrupt Hela, who dwells at the roots of the tree rather than its crown. Odin is 'not that kind of underworld god' and represents more immortalization through words and deeds, something a culture with Samurai could likely empathize with.<br /> <br /> Edit: After doing more research he apparently frequently does necromancy, so you've got me there, but the point stands that if the Kami hate him, it's probably not because he has a hard time respecting women. Hating his pantheon because they're lead by a necromancer is more than enough.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11726,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","user":"Solana","date":". 11-08-2016, 03:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T12:01:07-08:00","id":1000376,"title":"","content":"Not to mention, they're both from cultures that invented folding steel to deal with shortages of high-quality iron!<br /> <br /> Seriously, though, as far as Pantheon Leaders go, Odin is an extremely humble one, and there's considerable ethnographic and linguistic evidence to suggest that Odin was <i>not</i> the original chief god of the Germanic peoples. That honor likely goes to Tyr, and Odin was the rough analogue to Hermes and even considered something of a &quot;trickster&quot; god. You would struggle to find a more diplomatic head-of-state among the pantheons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T13:09:06-08:00","id":1000398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">He's a dick to his brother, there's no real evidence aside from the way he treats Loki that he is a huge dick on the scale that the other gods in various pantheons are. He's also the consummate diplomat. While Tyr could be said to represent the Eddard Stark approach to divine kingship, he ended up losing a hand for it, and many scholars argue that this was symbolic of Odin's rise to prominence among Norse peoples, because Odin is more than honorable, he's pragmatic. He has to remain on good terms with the Vanir, and arguably the Alfar (or Frey wouldn't have a hall in Ljossalfheim, playing at ambassador). Even his dickery with the Wall of Asgard could be explained as an attempt to save face with the other gods. His mother is a jotun, Thor's mother is a jotun, Skadi is a jotun who lives in Asgard, Odin's clearly not above being diplomatic with his enemy either. Also, Loki is his blood brother, so that's significant as well. Things only <i>really</i> start to escalate between them when Odin has a vision that Loki's children with Angrboda will be a threat to the Aesir, and abducts them. Loki responds by killing Baldur, and around and around we go.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, I'm not as good with Norse mythology as I am with the Japanese one, but I'm not quite seeing a lot of the stuff you're expounding here in how I read the Eddas. Odin is, to pick up that vocab, certainly a dick to everyone he can be a dick to. There's him raping Rind (not exactly his most feminist moment either, that one), there's his ridiculous and unnecessary flyting with Thor in the Harbardsljod, there's all those giants he has tricked for his selfish gain, and all those mortals he has tricked basically for fun.<br /> Then, I can't see Odin being diplomatic at all. He was the one to start the war with the Vanir, by casting the first spear. They only agreed on peace because no side could win. And Freyr isn't his diplomatic attach\u00e9 in Alfheim, but rather the elf-world was the fertility god's tooth-gift. The Aesir taking Jotun women has nothing to do with diplomacy either, it is rather a paradigm of enforced superiority: When Jotun try to take Aesir women, they are killed, but the Aesir freely take Jotun women as wives and lovers. That's the opposite of diplomacy, that's an allegory for the realities of raiding warfare.<br /> Lastly, there's the realm of scholarly theory. While Tyr could be a high god older than Odin himself, I personally know of no proof for that for the North Germanic peoples in particular (Freyr seems a more likely candidate for that to me personally, but as I said, it's not among my main areas of expertise), especially no foundation in mythology itself. We might go back and forth over religious history, but when mythology says Odin was king since the beginning of always, then a game based on mythology will depict him as such. The same goes for drawing a connection between the banishment of Loki's children and his murder of Baldr: It could make sense, but in the end it's pure speculation. The Norse myths as such stand very isolated from each other, finding a correct order for them is hard, if not even, due to their internal contradictions, impossible. While I'm more open for including scholarly theory in the depicitions of gods here in Scion, in general Scion 2.0 focusses very strongly on primary sources, and those folks have a point there: Theories change, speculation differs, but the primary sources stay the same. And those, at least taken alone, do not paint a flattering picture of Odin.<br /> Or, as Watcher put it in a chat with me: &quot;While I am not a follower of Rankean History, specifically with Scion, focusing on Primary Sources is really helpful when writing Pantheons, otherwise you end up in a bit of a confusing mess as you have to weigh individual arguments against each other. So, in the end, we use Primary Sources like canon.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;The other main form of Germanic shamanism is contained within the magical tradition known as </span></span></span><a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/concepts/seidr/\" target=\"_blank\">seidr</a><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">, of which Odin and Freya are the foremost divine practitioners. In traditional Germanic society, for a man to engage in seidr was effectively to forsake the male gender role, which brought considerable scorn upon any male who chose to take up this path. As the sagas show, this didn\u2019t stop some men from practicing seidr anyway. However, even Odin wasn\u2019t exempt from such charges of \u201cunmanliness,\u201d and was taunted for adopting the feminine traits and tasks that form part of the backbone of seidr. Saxo, in the passage on Odin\u2019s exile alluded to above, relates that \u201cby his stage-tricks and his assumption of a woman\u2019s work he had brought the foulest scandal on the name of the gods.\u201d</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">[16]</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"> Note also the reference to being \u201cfertilized\u201d in the verse quoted above \u2013 while this is certainly a metaphor, it\u2019s a metaphor loaded with sexual implications that would have been immediately recognizable to any Viking Age or medieval reader or hearer of the poem. A fuller discussion of the relationship between Germanic shamanism and gender roles can be found </span></span></span><a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/concepts/shamanism/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><span style=\"color:#2B2B2B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">. For our present purposes, it\u2019s sufficient to point out that, in the eyes of the pre-Christian northern Europeans, Odin\u2019s practice of seidr made him a rather \u201cunmanly\u201d being incapable of fulfilling the expectations placed upon an honorable man.&quot;</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://norse-mythology.org/gods-and-creatures/the-aesir-gods-and-goddesses/odin/\" target=\"_blank\">http://norse-mythology.org/gods-and-...oddesses/odin/</a> is where the quote is from, it's a fairly decent site.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it's an okay site alright, although the author could be a bit more careful with quotation - I've stumbled upon misquoted lines there already. But what they're writing there mostly agrees with what I know of <i>seidr</i>. Odin certainly does stints in female form, as a &quot;milkmaid&quot; even, accused by Loki of bearing children. But that doesn't mean he's exactly a feminist - there's lots of female conservatives who think women shouldn't lead, so why should a rapist like Odin suddenly become feminist just from temporarily changing his sex?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, but at this point you're arguing that any woman would have the same problem making a first impression with a pantheon lead by a woman, because Our Women Are Different\u2122. Even the Tuatha would lose their 'edge' because their notion of what a female leader is like would differ from what Japan believes a woman should be - to say nothing of the fact that you already expressed Amateratsu being out of sorts about Japan's stance on women. Why wouldn't she respect a culture where a man viewed her magic and shamanistic history as valuable enough to sacrifice his dignity and honor for? Why wouldn't she respect a culture where one of the chief war gods is a woman, and whose people actively pray to a Disir (Female Ancestor) to guide and protect them in battle?<br /> <br /> Again, it's not that I have an issue with the Kami and the Aesir not getting along, it's that you specifically used the example that they only really see Odin, and Odin has a problem with women leading.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh deary, Watcher brought up quite the complicated point. What he was getting at, basically, was the idea that gender is an artificial construct, which is one huge can of worms. Put simply, that means that by the standards of a Viking woman, a Japanese woman would not be a woman. Now that's putting it very flatly, it's extremely simplified, but the point is: Odin, or any Viking chief, has by virtue of the upbringing in his culture a very specific concept of what a woman is and how she behaves. And a Japanese empress or shaman would certainly not fit that. We're not saying that there has to automatically arise misunderstanding and disrespect from that (okay, we <i>are</i> saying that feudal Japan was extremely culture chauvinist, but that's beside the point right now), but the cultural differences, and Odin's dickish personality as argued above would certainly not make things easier. That's all we're saying.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We call both of them the same thing because they enjoy many similar facets. The Norse have Land Wights, the Japanese had spirits of the land worth placating and working with, just as norse shamans worked with their own indigenous spirits. There are going to be technical differences, because that's true of anything sharing a similar label across significant geographical difference, but we wouldn't assign them the same name if they weren't comparable ways of thinking.<br /> <br /> Also Odin would have absolutely no issue being taught a different way of doing things. He goes out of his way to learn the magic of every race he can, and again, sacrificed his dignity and honor in order to be 'put in his place' by a woman.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, Norse <i>seidr</i> and Japanese <i>miko</i>-shamanism are extremely different. The invocation of <i>landvaettir</i> (who, as far as I know, have no known connection to the <i>seidkona</i>) and the spirit-invocations of the <i>miko</i> (mostly concerned with the dead) are in no way comparable - saying they're both shamanism is like saying that the Aesir and the Kami are both gods, technically true, but not helpful. I won't dwell on the details, because both practices are very complicated and only fragmentarily attested, but I can only stress that literally the only thing they have in common is that some white, male scholar put a label reading &quot;shamanism&quot; on them.<br /> I would agree that Odin might be interested in learning Japanese shamanism, because he's interested in learning anything that increases his power, but that doesn't change that he would know absolutely nothing about how to behave when meeting a Japanese empress. In fact, his overt interest in hallowed, secret practices of the Kami, especially if he made the mistake of connecting it to <i>seidr</i> and thus to sexual acts, would be offensive in and off itself.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, plenty of people would argue that Odin is incredibly sympathetic to women, his most trusted servants, the valkyries, are women. Freya is their leader and arguably the leader of her group in Asgard because Njord is a bit of a pushover, honestly. The Jotuns are a matriarchal people (Angrboda leads those in the Ironwood, Loki takes the name of his mother 'Laufeysson'), so we also can't claim that he's never been exposed to women in power, to say nothing of his inability to topple Hela, who is queen of the 'other' afterlife.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, all I can say is that we're in the realm of opinions now - plenty of people would argue that Odin is incredibly sympathetic to women, but we are, as you've noticed, kinda argueing the opposite. The valkyries are weird, it's hard to tell if they're Odin's girls, or Freyja's girls, or Skuld's girls, or someone else's girls all together. The way their lore has been passed down to us obviously mixed a lot of things up, so I won't make any judgements on them. Freyja and Angrboda being leaders is speculation not found as such in the primary sources, so again, that's just not how we approach things. I can't see the Jotnar as matriarchal either, as a) Loki's matro<i>nymy </i>does not automatically imply matri<i>archy</i> and b) we see plenty of Jotun rulers with their female relatives relegated to the usual background role. While some Jotun like Skadi and the daughters of Geirrodr cerainly espouse the traits of the &quot;dangerous female&quot;, we would be hard-pressed to make out any actual women leaders in Norse mythology - except for Hel, whom you mentioned. She stands, I agree, and maybe Odin should be used to female authority from her - again, we come to opinions. We think the other points expounded here weigh more strongly in attesting Odin and Norse culture in general a patriarchal conception of gender, but you may make of the other points what you like. We hold both opinions to be valid in their own ways.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Norse pantheons are some of the most 'racially diverse' in the world, and Odin is a diplomat to basically every race in it, if any god can handle different customs of hospitality, it would be him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the &quot;racial diversity&quot; of the Norse pantheon (being composed of Aesir, Vanir and the odd half-Jotun), beings all conceived of by the same culture within the same frame of mind, is hardly comparable to the confrontation between actual, human cultural differences. I have argued against the image of Odin as diplomat above already, and here I can only add that, although Aesir, Vanir and Jotnar are different kinds of beings within Norse mythology, they are still all Norse and thus radically different from the deities of a culture half a world away. Even if we take a diplomatic angle on Odin, he would always be practicing Norse social norms when dealing with them, which would, as we think, mix very badly with the Japanse ones.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000359#post1000359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The ravens are symbols of higher consciousness and reality, representing thought and memory. They are carrion birds simply because carrion birds will gravitate to a battlefield, therefor they must be Odin's messengers. If anything the Kami would empathize with his constant struggle for dominion over death with the far more corrupt Hela, who dwells at the roots of the tree rather than its crown. Odin is 'not that kind of underworld god' and represents more immortalization through words and deeds, something a culture with Samurai could likely empathize with.</div> </div> </div> </div>All I can say about that is that the Kami would not care what Odin's death associations <i>symbolise</i> - they are still forms of <i>kegare</i>, ritual corruption or defilement. Corruption can't be compromised, it is a natural, physical thing, and death is the most aggressive form of it. It's poison to the Japanese ritual world, it inhibits the workings and powers of the Kami. Odin, that Hanged God, champion of execution and summoner of the dead, could argue about &quot;not being that kind of underworld god&quot; all he likes, his presence would still be foul to the Kami by principle.<br /> <br /> To finish all of this of, I have to say that I enjoy this kind of discussion, and I'd love to go over whatever points you still disagree with at length, but as long as we're mostly argueing over opinions and speculations that have little impact on our Kami Revision, I think it might be better if we don't further derail this thread with talk about the Aesir - it's supposed to be about the Kami, after all. Feel free to PM me - or, of course, to go on posting here, if you want to bring up further ideas for public discussion here, I'm after all not trying to police you.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000376#post1000376\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not to mention, they're both from cultures that invented folding steel to deal with shortages of high-quality iron!<br /> <br /> Seriously, though, as far as Pantheon Leaders go, Odin is an extremely humble one, and there's considerable ethnographic and linguistic evidence to suggest that Odin was <i>not</i> the original chief god of the Germanic peoples. That honor likely goes to Tyr, and Odin was the rough analogue to Hermes and even considered something of a &quot;trickster&quot; god. You would struggle to find a more diplomatic head-of-state among the pantheons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most of your points I've already addressed with my reply to Solana, but I'd like to add that a guy who risks his life in a contest of wisdom just to prove that he's better than some all-wise giant doesn't seem very humble to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T15:28:19-08:00","id":1000463,"title":"","content":"While this gets into the realm of opinion and speculation, I always enjoyed this discussion on the disposition of Odin from the <b>JSR Blog</b>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anne</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, Odin. Has there been a band of PCs yet that didn't hate him both in and out of character? (Trick question: most of them end up not hating him anymore in character because of social knack shenanigans, but that just makes their players hate him more in real life.)<br /> <br /> Being a dick is definitely part of the Odin's glorious and time-honored myths; it's easy to forget due to how nicely he's usually portrayed in modern sources (Marvel's Thor, in which he's generally shown to be a wise and benevolent old grandfather with everyone's best interests at heart, comes to mind), but this is a guy who habitually does things like disguise himself and refuse to help out his son while simultaneously telling him he's a cuckold, or digging up dead women to demand she tell him the future and then refusing to let her go back to sleep in spite of her begging until he gets what he wants. His dickery is pretty well-established, though like most gods he has many facets and is also positive in many of them. He's actually extremely similar to Loki in personality; the only real difference between them before Ragnarok is that Odin is the king and Loki is dependent on his goodwill.<br /> <br /> So yes, to a great extent he's such a bastard because, well, that's what he does in myth, too. He's a trickster god; he just happens to be in the surprising position of ruler, which most tricksters aren't. Most other pantheon heads are associated with things like law, order and justice, the basic foundations that keep society functioning; Odin does do those things, but he also directly subverts them when he feels like it.<br /> <br /> For us, Odin also represents a lot of the Aesir values and culture, which, while very fun to write metal songs about and watch in movies, are actually pretty horrible for the people they're happening to. As any of the Vanir could tell you, the Aesir are a race of raiders and conquerors, which means that they're inevitably going to make enemies when they roll into other peoples' territories and start looting all the wenches and ale. Odin's at the forefront of those sorts of things as the god of war, and having Scions in the world through which he can work is the perfect chance for him to get to exercise those tendencies after the gods' long absence.<br /> <br /> Finally, of course, Odin is an increasingly desperate god, and desperate people do desperate things. His death has been foretold, his attempts to circumvent it are doomed to failure, and he's facing not only his death but the deaths of most of his friends and children, the betrayal of his blood-brother and the destruction of everything he's built over the centuries. Even if he weren't already the kind of guy who throws his friend's kids in jail for looking creepy or goes to other peoples' houses in disguise for the express purpose of pranking them, that's the kind of motivator that makes people do insane things, and he's been living with it for a very, very long time. It's quite likely that for Odin, the ends justify any and all means if it gives him even the longest shot at averting or delaying the end.<br /> <br /> None of this means that Odin can't do good things, of course. He certainly can, and has, even in our games. But, as almost all gods in Scion continually illustrate, doing good things does not necessarily make you a good person; he's fully capable of taking care of people he cares about while being the biggest bastard in Asgard to someone else, often at exactly the same time.</div> </div> </div> </div>And...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anne</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for Odin... well, as you all know, Odin is more than a little bit of a humongous dick. This extends to his children as well as to everyone else; you only have to look at the stories of him cheerfully punking Thor's face off in disguise or nodding sagely as Tyr gets his hand bitten off to know that. That doesn't mean he doesn't care about them, of course, but he's always been very much about his own schemes first and foremost, and everybody's welfare somewhere a distant second. He would undoubtedly want to help his kids when they need it, provided he can do so without some kind of heinous penalty, but if there's something else he needs to be doing or helping them out would be just too much of a pain in the ass, he's likely to let them figure it out themselves rather than stepping in. He's trying to simultaneously avert Ragnarok, hold onto his own power and still have time for prankship along the way; he ain't got time for your problems right now, Bragi.<br /> <br /> Of course, if you asked him, I'm sure he would tell you that averting Ragnarok is in everyone's best interests and he is therefore constantly working to safeguard his people. It just happens to come with the delicious side benefit of himself not getting eaten by a giant wolf. He's an altruist!</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T16:25:57-08:00","id":1000500,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sacerdos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000398#post1000398\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Odin is, to pick up that vocab, certainly a dick to everyone he can be a dick to. There's him raping Rind (not exactly his most feminist moment either, that one),</div> </div> </div> </div>You'll note that I said 'huge dick relative to other gods'. Every single god in ancient world mythology is going to be a huge dick because they're over 1,000 years outdated. Which I'll get back into later on in response to one of your comments. This in mind, when I said Odin is 'not that much of a dick relative to other gods' it means he doesn't go around murdering every Jotun he sees like Thor does, or play Devil's Advocate the way Loki does. Honestly the only Aesir who is objectively a better person than Odin is Tyr, the rest are either worse or simply don't appear nearly often enough in the lore we have today to be a comparison.<br /> <br /> I'm especially confused by your comment that it wasn't 'a very feminist moment' for Odin. That's kind of a loaded theory, Amateratsu is not going to be a feminist either by the philosophy's standards. Moreover, unlike Zeus, Odin doesn't just rape her on a whim. He does it for a very specific reason that has nothing to do with simply disrespecting her as a woman.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">After </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baldr\" target=\"_blank\">Balderus</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">' death Odin consulted seers on how to get revenge. On their advice Odin went to the Ruthenians disguised as a warrior called Roster. There he was twice turned down by Rinda. He then wrote runes on a piece of bark and touched her with it, causing her to go mad, and disguised himself as a medicine woman called Wecha, who was allowed to see her. Finally she fell ill; the disguised Odin then said he had medicine with which to cure her but that it would cause a violent reaction. On Odin's advice, the king tied Rinda to her bed, and Odin proceeded to rape her. From the rape was born Bous, who would later avenge Balderus.</span></span></span><sup><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rindr#cite_note-Lindow-1\" target=\"_blank\">[1]</a></sup><sup><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rindr#cite_note-Simek-2\" target=\"_blank\">[2]</a></sup><sup><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rindr#cite_note-DeVries-3\" target=\"_blank\">[3]</a></sup> </div> </div> </div>This is probably one of the most egregious things he's ever been recounted doing, and it's no surprise that it happens after the death of his favorite son. The other, arguably most horrible thing, is when he turns one of Loki's Aesir sons into a wolf, who then disembowels his brother, so he can bind Loki with those same entrails. The moral of the story is that Odin is not a guy whose son you want to murder. Does that justify his actions? No, but it does make your somewhat callous version of him a bit at odds with the 'canon', because the context is important when determining if this was just something he'd do on a whim, or something he'd do under a specific set of circumstances.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> there's his ridiculous and unnecessary flyting with Thor in the Harbardsljod, </div> </div> </div>If anyone deserves to get schooled in a rap battle, it's Thor, guy can use the exercise of walking around the river anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> there's all those giants he has tricked for his selfish gain, and all those mortals he has tricked basically for fun. </div> </div> </div>I can think of one immediate example in the giant that was hired to build the wall of Asgard, and the deception was a dickish move but hey, also they need a wall, and the guy isn't going to take any payment except Freya. You could 'speculate' (because I know how much you love that word) that Odin deceived the guy because demanding Freya as a form of payment was ultimately an asshole thing to do, and thus isn't worth being honest toward, but at the end of the day it ends up not mattering because as the edda states - the Aesir wanted to be pulled out of 'their' mess by Loki. You'll note it says 'the Aesir' and not 'Odin went to his brother after fucking up', because the Aesir are hot-blooded folk that have already shown they are going to question Odin's ability to lead or even be an honorable man. He <i>has</i> to cleave to some conceptions because going against the will of your keys to power is a good way to get ousted (like arguably Tyr did over time, but I understand we're ignoring that).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then, I can't see Odin being diplomatic at all. He was the one to start the war with the Vanir, by casting the first spear. </div> </div> </div>The war was already all but started when Freya riled up the Aesir and then made them reflect on how their pursuit of her was making them tarnish their own virtues, and they tried to kill her multiple times. Again, Odin may have declared war on the Vanir, but whether he did or not the Vanir were already coming for the Aesir, to <i>not</i> declare war would be to admit weakness as a leader and to basically tear his own people apart, when in fact Odin will do literally anything to protect their future.<br /> <br /> This is why I don't accept your idea that speculation is wrong and we should just take what is literally said and make it canon. First of all - what is written is the product of Christians with an axe to grind against paganism, at literally the twilight, the dying breath, of that religion in the first place. Second of all - it's not very compelling, is it?<br /> <br /> &quot;Odin declared war on the Vanir because he's a dick and that's what vikings do!&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Odin declared war on the Vanir because of a complicated political climate and because it was the pragmatic thing to do as 'king' of the gods.&quot;<br /> <br /> One of these options leads to intrigue, the possibility for complex relationships between gods, and allows for a relatable and engaging character, you know, the back bone of good storytelling? The other one offers you a chief of the gods who has no humanity and is just a villain.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They only agreed on peace because no side could win. </div> </div> </div>... Okay? Is Odin more of a dick than other gods now, because he knows when to quit? I'm sure Zeus would be much better at admitting a stalemate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And Freyr isn't his diplomatic attach\u00e9 in Alfheim, but rather the elf-world was the fertility god's tooth-gift. </div> </div> </div>And I'm sure he never serves in an ambassadorial capacity between Ljossalfheim and Asgard because there's no epic poem about his doing boring ambassadorial work with a race that barely has recorded myths (comparable to other groups) in the mythology already.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Aesir taking Jotun women has nothing to do with diplomacy either, it is rather a paradigm of enforced superiority: When Jotun try to take Aesir women, they are killed, but the Aesir freely take Jotun women as wives and lovers. That's the opposite of diplomacy, that's an allegory for the realities of raiding warfare. </div> </div> </div>Skadi earns her place in Asgard independent of her status as a woman or as a Jotun, and demands to take a husband, and Odin lets her. Frey has to 'earn' his Ettin-bride by literally giving up his sword to her. Jotun women are not waifs, and while Odin might boast about his conquests, it's just as much speculation to assert he went out and raped all of them as it is to assert any of the things I've been speculating in my posts.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lastly, there's the realm of scholarly theory. While Tyr could be a high god older than Odin himself, I personally know of no proof for that for the North Germanic peoples in particular (Freyr seems a more likely candidate for that to me personally, but as I said, it's not among my main areas of expertise), especially no foundation in mythology itself. </div> </div> </div>Okay then. . . maybe do some research instead of just making an argument from authority that you don't cite and then say: &quot;My own feelings think Frey makes more sense for that, but whatever.&quot; Jachra already presented the argument for why Tyr was likely the original king before Odin became the king figure in the telling, it's a better argument than: &quot;This isn't my specialty, I don't know where you're getting that from, and I think it's wrong.&quot;<br /> <br /> You know no proof of it, which obviously means the point is wrong or invalid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We might go back and forth over religious history, but when mythology says Odin was king since the beginning of always, then a game based on mythology will depict him as such. </div> </div> </div>Argument wasn't that the lore should show Tyr as the original king unseated for sake of Odin, argument was that, over time, the Norse peoples stopped considering Tyr to be the king figure in favor of another god because he is 'more pragmatic', Odin is the pragmatic leader, not the Eddard Stark one. No where in that point is it said that Tyr ought to be the king of the gods in this game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The same goes for drawing a connection between the banishment of Loki's children and his murder of Baldr: It could make sense, but in the end it's pure speculation. </div> </div> </div>Again, the alternative is just assuming the gods do things for no reason, which is neither compelling nor interesting, and doesn't really offer anything to a story except for maybe make someone feel good about how much they refused to deviate from the absolute most literal interpretation of what some guys said about another people's culture.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Norse myths as such stand very isolated from each other, finding a correct order for them is hard, if not even, due to their internal contradictions, impossible. While I'm more open for including scholarly theory in the depicitions of gods here in Scion, in general Scion 2.0 focusses very strongly on primary sources, and those folks have a point there: Theories change, speculation differs, but the primary sources stay the same. And those, at least taken alone, do not paint a flattering picture of Odin. </div> </div> </div>They also don't paint a very compelling picture of any pantheon in general. You're basically calling for the calcification of myth in storytelling and that's not something I can support in the slightest, if that's what you're going for, all the power in the world to you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or, as Watcher put it in a chat with me: &quot;While I am not a follower of Rankean History, specifically with Scion, focusing on Primary Sources is really helpful when writing Pantheons, otherwise you end up in a bit of a confusing mess as you have to weigh individual arguments against each other. So, in the end, we use Primary Sources like canon.&quot; </div> </div> </div>I'm sure it makes it easier to write, but it's probably not going to lead to the best possible product. Also none of the sources for Mythology can be considered 'Primary sources', they're all written by Christian guys who are talking about what they claim to have heard from some other guy. So... Much of Norse 'primary sources' are already 'speculation' between scholars, how do you pick which primary source is 'right'?<br /> <br /> Yeah, it's an okay site alright, although the author could be a bit more careful with quotation - I've stumbled upon misquoted lines there already. But what they're writing there mostly agrees with what I know of <i>seidr</i>. Odin certainly does stints in female form, as a &quot;milkmaid&quot; even, accused by Loki of bearing children. But that doesn't mean he's exactly a feminist - there's lots of female conservatives who think women shouldn't lead, so why should a rapist like Odin suddenly become feminist just from temporarily changing his sex?<br /> <br /> Milk maid, medicine woman, seidr practioner, it's all gravy to him, apparently. Which brings us back to the original point - why would Odin have a problem respecting a leader because she is a woman? He gives half of the dead to Freya even 'claims' half the Einherjar the valkyries collect. At the end of the day, Odin still had no problems putting himself in a subservient position to a woman, as her student.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh deary, Watcher brought up quite the complicated point. What he was getting at, basically, was the idea that gender is an artificial construct, which is one huge can of worms. Put simply, that means that by the standards of a Viking woman, a Japanese woman would not be a woman. </div> </div> </div>I don't know if you meant 'Oh deary' to sound condescending, but it did.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now that's putting it very flatly, it's extremely simplified, but the point is: Odin, or any Viking chief, has by virtue of the upbringing in his culture a very specific concept of what a woman is and how she behaves. And a Japanese empress or shaman would certainly not fit that. We're not saying that there has to automatically arise misunderstanding and disrespect from that (okay, we <i>are</i> saying that feudal Japan was extremely culture chauvinist, but that's beside the point right now), but the cultural differences, and Odin's dickish personality as argued above would certainly not make things easier. That's all we're saying. </div> </div> </div>And now you're basically just restating what he said as though I don't understand it. It completely fails to address my point, which is that going out of his way to say that Odin would have a problem respecting women in the first place, and then justifying it further by saying &quot;Oh well sure I mean Odin has a pretty good perspective on what it means to be a woman in Norse mythology, and respect them enough to take female form/learn women's magic at expense to himself, but he doesn't understand 'Japanese' women.&quot;<br /> <br /> Which makes no god damned sense as a counter argument, because <i>any woman from any culture that is not Japan is therefor going to have difficulty respecting a female leader because even if they understand and respect women Japanese women are different</i>.<br /> <br /> This write up even bears mention about female warriors, norse mythology has Shield-Maidens, the Disir, there have been female thanes, etc. I'm having a hard time seeing why Amateratsu, who is portrayed as potentially being at odds with her own pantheon, would not find something to respect in a pantheon where women demand husbands, where valkyries are some of the most ferocious warriors in the cosmology, and an entire race of Matriarchal beings exists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Actually, Norse <i>seidr</i> and Japanese <i>miko</i>-shamanism are extremely different. The invocation of <i>landvaettir</i> (who, as far as I know, have no known connection to the <i>seidkona</i>) and the spirit-invocations of the <i>miko</i> (mostly concerned with the dead) are in no way comparable - saying they're both shamanism is like saying that the Aesir and the Kami are both gods, technically true, but not helpful. I won't dwell on the details, because both practices are very complicated and only fragmentarily attested, but I can only stress that literally the only thing they have in common is that some white, male scholar put a label reading &quot;shamanism&quot; on them. </div> </div> </div>Except it's easy to find similarities in shrines to land spirits/gods of the land, and shrines to appease land wights, that they also stress the importance of honoring your ancestors for strength, that Japan's shamans were almost exclusively female, and Seidr is a female or emsculating practice. These are points they have in common even if they are not exactly the same thing. No one in this thread ever argued that Norse shamanistic practices were <i>identical</i> to Japanese shamanism.<br /> <br /> Also Seidr isn't the only practice of shamanism in Norse Culture, so that's a thing worth pointing out, since you worded your comment like it's the only way.<br /> <br /> It's also pretty funny that this post talks about just how infinitely important it is to use primary sources like a bible, but you can't accept that shamanism was a label assigned to both groups, after all, they were written by <i>old white men.</i> You know, those same old white men who were Christian and didn't entirely understand heathenism, or else outright vilified it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would agree that Odin might be interested in learning Japanese shamanism, because he's interested in learning anything that increases his power, but that doesn't change that he would know absolutely nothing about how to behave when meeting a Japanese empress. In fact, his overt interest in hallowed, secret practices of the Kami, especially if he made the mistake of connecting it to <i>seidr</i> and thus to sexual acts, would be offensive in and off itself. </div> </div> </div>Why would Odin make that mistake? Is he an idiot? Because in First Edition he is one of the most intelligent gods, and also has the Mystery purview.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, all I can say is that we're in the realm of opinions now - plenty of people would argue that Odin is incredibly sympathetic to women, but we are, as you've noticed, kinda argueing the opposite. The valkyries are weird, it's hard to tell if they're Odin's girls, or Freyja's girls, or Skuld's girls, or someone else's girls all together. The way their lore has been passed down to us obviously mixed a lot of things up, so I won't make any judgements on them. </div> </div> </div>Even if he's not sympathetic to them (which is fine if you think that, even though it doesn't make a whole lot of sense for someone to spend a lot of time turning into women, demeaning himself for woman's magic, and so on, without being able to see them as actual people) the argument is that he would have no issue respecting a female leader, which as I pointed out already, is demonstrated in your primary sources.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Freyja and Angrboda being leaders is speculation not found as such in the primary sources, so again, that's just not how we approach things. I can't see the Jotnar as matriarchal either, as a) Loki's matro<i>nymy </i>does not automatically imply matri<i>archy</i> and b) we see plenty of Jotun rulers with their female relatives relegated to the usual background role. </div> </div> </div>And yet taking your mother's name as a boy is a perilously odd thing to do in norse society, figures like Skadi exist, and Angrboda being the hag of the Ironwood is fairly telling when you consider what that word means in the context of the stories. It also never explicitly says that they aren't matriarchal, but by going the opposite direction you're... still taking that approach, it's entirely a matter of personal preference, and appealing to primary source doesn't work here. Maybe speculation is a good thing!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While some Jotun like Skadi and the daughters of Geirrodr cerainly espouse the traits of the &quot;dangerous female&quot;, we would be hard-pressed to make out any actual women leaders in Norse mythology - except for Hel, whom you mentioned. She stands, I agree, and maybe Odin should be used to female authority from her - again, we come to opinions. We think the other points expounded here weigh more strongly in attesting Odin and Norse culture in general a patriarchal conception of gender, but you may make of the other points what you like. We hold both opinions to be valid in their own ways. </div> </div> </div>It's an opinion that directly flies in the face of your primary source, though. You've chosen to veer from that because the idea appeals to you. Which is why it seems so condescending when your argument uses 'Primary Sources' as an unshakeable foundation. It lends the impression that your speculation is the only time it's allowed.<br /> <br /> Also Norse society and mythology are, as this entire discussion illustrated, vastly more feminist than Japan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, the &quot;racial diversity&quot; of the Norse pantheon (being composed of Aesir, Vanir and the odd half-Jotun), beings all conceived of by the same culture within the same frame of mind, is hardly comparable to the confrontation between actual, human cultural differences. I have argued against the image of Odin as diplomat above already, and here I can only add that, although Aesir, Vanir and Jotnar are different kinds of beings within Norse mythology, they are still all Norse and thus radically different from the deities of a culture half a world away. Even if we take a diplomatic angle on Odin, he would always be practicing Norse social norms when dealing with them, which would, as we think, mix very badly with the Japanse ones. </div> </div> </div>Aesir, Jotnar, Vanir, Alfar (both kinds), Duergar, other things besides, and a spiritual system that literally requires you negotiate with spirits in order to get what you want. I never said that any of their groups or societies are at all like the ones in Japan, but it does mean that Odin is used to dealing with people who are not of his race, who don't have the same understanding of honor he does, and who he often has to work with because - as mentioned - he can't just fucking wipe the Vanir, Jotnar, or Alfar off the map. You know, diplomacy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All I can say about that is that the Kami would not care what Odin's death associations <i>symbolise</i> - they are still forms of <i>kegare</i>, ritual corruption or defilement. Corruption can't be compromised, it is a natural, physical thing, and death is the most aggressive form of it. It's poison to the Japanese ritual world, it inhibits the workings and powers of the Kami. Odin, that Hanged God, champion of execution and summoner of the dead, could argue about &quot;not being that kind of underworld god&quot; all he likes, his presence would still be foul to the Kami by principle. </div> </div> </div>I already conceded the point, the Kami would hate Odin because he's a Necromancer, he does it because he believes that the ends justify the means, but they're obviously not going to care about that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To finish all of this of, I have to say that I enjoy this kind of discussion, and I'd love to go over whatever points you still disagree with at length, but as long as we're mostly argueing over opinions and speculations that have little impact on our Kami Revision, I think it might be better if we don't further derail this thread with talk about the Aesir - it's supposed to be about the Kami, after all. Feel free to PM me - or, of course, to go on posting here, if you want to bring up further ideas for public discussion here, I'm after all not trying to police you. </div> </div> </div>Still arguing that it's not even just opinion or speculation, and it's still directly relevant to your write up of the Kami in how they'd regard Odin. It's nice that you've gone out of your way to say that you're not trying to police me, but you basically just told me to take my argument against a design decision your group made for your write up, and then put it somewhere private where other people won't have to witness those criticisms, which feels a little dishonest.<br /> <br /> It also subtly communicates to me the following: &quot;Your discussion has no business in our thread, please take it elsewhere sir/madam.&quot; I don't know that you meant it that way, and I certainly don't think Watcher would be intentionally dismissive, but it makes it hard to speak from a respectful place when you feel like your own position is being repeatedly dismissed for A) being speculation (but like, different than our speculation) and B) being either caustic to discussion in the thread or simply having no business being heard within it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most of your points I've already addressed with my reply to Solana, but I'd like to add that a guy who risks his life in a contest of wisdom just to prove that he's better than some all-wise giant doesn't seem very humble to me. </div> </div> </div>You're referring to Mimir? 'cause - yeah, that's not at all why he did that, actually. He wanted to ask Mimir questions, the price of this was a game of wits, and if Odin lost he'd lose his own head. See how that's meant to be poetic, instead of just an event for the sake of having an event, and how it ties into Odin's 'change destiny even if it kills me' attitude?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11726,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","user":"Solana","date":". 11-08-2016, 07:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T16:42:40-08:00","id":1000514,"title":"","content":"Solona, I don't know if we've done something to offend you, but you are being incredibly harsh and aggressive. We spent almost a year researching this, and have written an absolute ton. I can see we disagree on the nature of Odin, and that's fine. We have no intention of telling you that your own interpretation of the All Father is wrong, just explaining how we got to our own conclusions on him.<br /> <br /> But, this thread is supposed to be for the Kami, you disagree with two sentences we wrote, that's cool, totally fine. We stand by our interpretation, but, this a lot, and more harsh than I am comfortable with over those two sentences. <br /> <br /> I stand by everything we have said, but I don't have an interest in discussing things with this degree of harshness over two sentences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T17:49:25-08:00","id":1000551,"title":"","content":"I think this goes under the heading of &quot;play it the way you like&quot;, because the handed-down myths of Odin leave it open to interpretation. This isn't mythology class - it's a game, and this is a (very cool and well-researched) version of the Kami. Use what you like, change what you don't. Bravo to the Scion 2.0 team - will there be more in the future?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T17:59:41-08:00","id":1000558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000551#post1000551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this goes under the heading of &quot;play it the way you like&quot;, because the handed-down myths of Odin leave it open to interpretation. This isn't mythology class - it's a game, and this is a (very cool and well-researched) version of the Kami. Use what you like, change what you don't. Bravo to the Scion 2.0 team - will there be more in the future?</div> </div> </div> </div>I hope there will be more in the future. At the moment our little group can't exactly decide if we want to keep working on things for 1e, or wait for 2e to come out and start writing up things for that. I will probably set my eyes on a new personal project for 1e before then, since I research these to relax. But, I am not entirely sure what I want to work on next. I have been reading Wyandot (Get named the Huron by Samuel de Champlain) mythology which I really quite like, but there is not a huge amount of actions the 'main cast' preform. Essentially, they have an Animal Council who could make great Major Deities, but only a few of those members of the council have enough deeds to be fleshed out.<br /> <br /> I have also been thinking about the Basque Pantheon, but from the little I have seen, we have even less than the Gauls on them. I would really love to do them one day though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T18:09:08-08:00","id":1000561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000514#post1000514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solona, I don't know if we've done something to offend you, but you are being incredibly harsh and aggressive. We spent almost a year researching this, and have written an absolute ton. I can see we disagree on the nature of Odin, and that's fine. We have no intention of telling you that your own interpretation of the All Father is wrong, just explaining how we got to our own conclusions on him.<br /> <br /> But, this thread is supposed to be for the Kami, you disagree with two sentences we wrote, that's cool, totally fine. We stand by our interpretation, but, this a lot, and more harsh than I am comfortable with over those two sentences.<br /> <br /> I stand by everything we have said, but I don't have an interest in discussing things with this degree of harshness over two sentences.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm seconding Watcher on this. If there's going to be a debate on the nature of Odin (and Odin's level of asshole-ness) please take it to another thread and do not derail the year of work and research put into a pantheon that has nothing to do with the Aesir. The level of hostility is not welcomed, and is a little derailing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000551#post1000551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this goes under the heading of &quot;play it the way you like&quot;, because the handed-down myths of Odin leave it open to interpretation. This isn't mythology class - it's a game, and this is a (very cool and well-researched) version of the Kami. Use what you like, change what you don't. Bravo to the Scion 2.0 team - will there be more in the future?</div> </div> </div> </div>We'd like to do more in the future, but grad school applications, a new job, and running into the last year of BSN Nursing School has tightened up our schedules considerably. We've been eyeballing ideas about our next project, and whiffling on if we want to wait for a full release of Scion 2nd Edition before we do a go-ahead.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T18:10:03-08:00","id":1000562,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000551#post1000551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this goes under the heading of &quot;play it the way you like&quot;, because the handed-down myths of Odin leave it open to interpretation. This isn't mythology class - it's a game, and this is a (very cool and well-researched) version of the Kami. Use what you like, change what you don't. Bravo to the Scion 2.0 team - will there be more in the future?</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Play it the way you like&quot; is something we can definitely agree on.<br /> <br /> We make a strong point of sticking to accurate and first hand (or as close to first hand) material as we can find, but what actually happens at the gaming table is entirely in the hands of all the awesome storytellers out there. Does this create a calcified depiction of mythology? Maybe. But we strongly feel that having this information arms everyone with the tools to make their own choices, as opposed to not having the information at all. We welcome everyone to take the pieces they enjoy and use them in their own games, or tell amazing stories with their own ideas and theories!<br /> <br /> As for the future, we're definitely going to do more! We're currently discussing if we want to wait until after the new edition of Scion is officially released before any more BIG projects.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":484,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","user":"Iry","date":". 11-08-2016, 09:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T18:25:24-08:00","id":1000565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000500#post1000500\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Still arguing that it's not even just opinion or speculation, and it's still directly relevant to your write up of the Kami in how they'd regard Odin. It's nice that you've gone out of your way to say that you're not trying to police me, but you basically just told me to take my argument against a design decision your group made for your write up, and then put it somewhere private where other people won't have to witness those criticisms, which feels a little dishonest.<br /> <br /> It also subtly communicates to me the following: &quot;Your discussion has no business in our thread, please take it elsewhere sir/madam.&quot; I don't know that you meant it that way, and I certainly don't think Watcher would be intentionally dismissive, but it makes it hard to speak from a respectful place when you feel like your own position is being repeatedly dismissed for A) being speculation (but like, different than our speculation) and B) being either caustic to discussion in the thread or simply having no business being heard within it.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This part comes off incredibly hostile and really disrespectful. This isn't just a &quot;write up&quot; for &quot;our group&quot;. It's over a year's worth of work and dedicated research that Watcher, Sacerdos, and Iry put hours and sweat and long, LONG hours of debate and discussion so we could put it out for the public Scion forums because we love this community and the community needed a Kami resource desperately.<br /> <br /> If you are sincerely passionate about the Odin discussion, a separate thread would serve to keep the target focused on Odin and the Aesir themselves and allow discussion of Odin and his portrayal without derailing from the Kami write up itself AND allow your own concerns and viewpoints to be addressed.<br /> <br /> As an ending point. We are not the <i>kata</i> for running the Kami. We do not expect there to be only one way of running the Kami. We have offered this as a guideline and a solid gathering of awesomeness that lazy STs like me love for running our games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T20:59:44-08:00","id":1000604,"title":"","content":"I feel like people are giving Odin too much credit, honestly.<br /> <br /> Odin was a manipulative asshole, end of story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T01:12:54-08:00","id":1000658,"title":"","content":"I would like to say that while Watcher and Aynie might have taken offense with the tone of the reply, it's not like Solana is talking completely unreasonably here or anything. Obviously, some things I said have come across very wrong. Solana, I'm sorry if anything I wrote got you personally riled up. I'm sorry if I seemed condescending, I didn't intend to, and I hope I won't make that impression on you again (the &quot;oh deary&quot;, in fact, was meant more as &quot;oh deary, now I'll have to dwell on constructivism <i>again</i>, I hate dwelling on constructivism&quot;). Regarding the &quot;policing&quot;, I also have to make amends: As others have pointed out already, I should have suggested another thread instead of a PM; I don't particularly like posting on forums, and am still not quite used to the etiquette and mechanics of doing so, so please forgive me on that. It was not about silencing your concerns, because addressing mythological accuracy is very serious to our project, but about keeping this thread on-topic.<br /> <br /> That's why I won't reply to your post point by point now, although you are, as I've tried to stress, more than welcome to continue the arguement somewhere else. I tried to write that reply quickly yesterday evening, trying to get it out before the discussion could move on somewhere else (an empty concern as I realise now), I'm sure we can solve a lot of our misunderstandings by getting more nitty-gritty with our source materials than I managed to do yesterday. Maybe I'll convince you after all. Maybe you'll convince me. But for now, our write-up will stay as it is.<br /> <br /> I'd still, in the end, like to make a note on research and sources, because that's obviously important to the approach of Scion 2.0 as a whole. You said I used the word &quot;speculation&quot; too often, and maybe I did. It comes from working in academia - you can't say half a thing before they rip you for speculating there. That doesn't mean well-founded speculation is a bad thing, and yes, while we think our reading is closer to the original meaning of the primary sources, that is still interpretation, and maybe even speculation. I never meant to say that your interpretation or speculation is invalid, but what I did mean to say was that neither interpretation nor speculation can really <i>be argued</i>. We're not saying what you got from reading the sources is wrong, but we kinda got the feeling, especially from the passionate way you addressed it, that you were saying that ours was, and that you wanted it changed. It's probably just another misunderstanding, and I hope this solves it. And while you are absolutely right that the recorders of Norse myth were falsifying it (although it could be argued that the Poetic Edda is far more authentic), it's not like the fact that Scion 2.0 is sticking to primary sources means we just take them at face-value, and act as if we hadn't noticed the obvious Christian bias. In interpreting a historical source, we of course try to understand and examine the authors intentions and agenda. That doesn't mean that someone like Snorri is equal to a 19th-century scholar creating an unhelpful category like &quot;shamanism&quot;. Snorri was from the culture he's writing about, and while he's obviously attempting to euhemerise and Christianise his past, he also obviously deeply adores it, or otherwise he wouldn't have tried to &quot;save the face&quot; of those gods and heroes by making them mortal kings instead of demons. He also quotes liberally from authentic, pagan sources that are lost to us, so you really can't call drawing on him (or others like him in the context of other pantheons) equivalent to speculation, or to drawing on the theories and hypotheses of scholars and enthusiasts.<br /> <br /> A last word maybe on what I <i>do</i> know about the Norse, because you seemed to call that into question entirely at some points (again, maybe I just misunderstood you). Now, as I said, the Norse are not my main area of expertise, and Scion 2.0 hasn't done a full rewrite of them yet, so opinions might change as we go deeper and learn more. But that doesn't mean we're just making uneducated gut-decisions either. I've worked through both Eddas a time or two, I've read Beowulf and some Sagas, and maybe about twenty titles of academic writing on Norse mythology. I'm assuming about the same for the members of Scion 2.0 itself. That's not a lot, and, as I pointed out, it doesn't compare to what I actually consider a topic I've researched in depth (namely Japan, on which my personal bibliography counts about 160 titles and 2 uni courses), but it's not like we've had a cursory look at Snorri and ran away with it either. So when I say &quot;Freyr seems a more likely candidate for original Scandinavian high god to me&quot;, I mean &quot;I've read some scholarly work on it, and its arguments have mostly convinced me&quot;. When I say the opposite of Tyr, I mean &quot;there seems evidence both archaeological and linguistic that suggest such a standing amongst the ancient continental Germanic peoples, but I've yet to see comparable scientific proof for Scandinavia&quot;. As I said, I think we've both made a number of false assumptions about each other. Please understand that nothing we have written here is drawn from thin air, every word and idea has been researched and argued over - and on many points we still don't agree, but we've made our compromises to present things the most reasonable and accurate way we could. Because this is (unlike how we treat primary sources, one might say) not a definite canon - but it is the product of our research and of our application of scientific method and academic research to the treatment of mythology.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're referring to Mimir? 'cause - yeah, that's not at all why he did that, actually. He wanted to ask Mimir questions, the price of this was a game of wits, and if Odin lost he'd lose his own head. See how that's meant to be poetic, instead of just an event for the sake of having an event, and how it ties into Odin's 'change destiny even if it kills me' attitude? </div> </div> </div>I was referring to Vafthrudnir, and from the way you tell the story, I guess you mean the same poem. And that contests pretty certainly wasn't about Odin learning something, because he states in the first stanza already that he wants to &quot;seek[s] to match&quot; Vafthrudnir's knowledge. Oh, and because I just noticed it now, you keep speeling it &quot;Amateratsu&quot;, but it actually is &quot;Amaterasu&quot;. Not a good point to address that, I guess, but better than the others? Anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T07:14:10-08:00","id":1000764,"title":"","content":"It's also pretty much impossible <i>not</i> to speculate when writing about mythology. So much is guesswork, reconstruction, interpretation, and filtering through the bias of later narrators (especially with the Norse and Vanir), that differences in details are inevitable. But this is about a game, and this thread is about one particular Kami rewrite for that game - and for my purposes, this version works very well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T07:28:38-08:00","id":1000771,"title":"","content":"I am glad you like the Kami rewrite Purple Snit! And everyone else who dies. I was honestly expecting a lot of fighting on Susano-O and Storms, or Hachiman and the nature of his War-'stuff'. But, it seems that no one seems to disparaged about them right now, which is great.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T08:10:59-08:00","id":1000792,"title":"","content":"Nope, no worried about the Susano-o/Hachiman changes. I figure if the research doesn't support them, then that's fine with me, and if I really need Susano-o to be a Storm God, I can go right ahead and do so in my games, and no-one's going to tell me I can't. Any chance of a rewrite for the Shen? Oh, and I wondered - no Pantheon-specific Purview for the Kami?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T08:33:50-08:00","id":1000810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000792#post1000792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope, no worried about the Susano-o/Hachiman changes. I figure if the research doesn't support them, then that's fine with me, and if I really need Susano-o to be a Storm God, I can go right ahead and do so in my games, and no-one's going to tell me I can't. Any chance of a rewrite for the Shen? Oh, and I wondered - no Pantheon-specific Purview for the Kami?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Susano-O stuff can be a good way to showcase the effects of Fate if you want to do that too. Essentially, a Victorian scholar doing one of the first translations of the Kojiki, I believe, misunderstood the text and believed Susano-O was a Storm God. His translation then goes on to form the backbone of most Western work and understanding, so this one little error gets repeated over, and over, and over, so much so that it is compounded into the base cultural consciousness. So, it could be an interesting way to showcase Fate messing with someone's Purviews.<br /> <br /> I would really like to do the Shen, and I have some brief notes on them, and have read several books on them under the guidance of Sacerdos. Really interesting stuff, so, they may come up eventually? I like their organization and structure, it is very nice compared to the Tuatha.<br /> <br /> The Kami PsP is actually entirely written, but a bit of a mess. It is in the process of being balanced, rebalanced, and having the &quot;Watcher-Ideas&quot; taken out, powers that are super accurate, but utterly useless just because what they do doesn't work amazingly in Scion, or players may not grasp the significance of.<br /> <br /> A little hint for what we have though is that it is based off ritual behavior to start with. Ritual purity, invoking Kami (or yourself being invoked) for help, that sort of thing. Demigod starts giving you special cult locals, and it grows from there to upper theological concepts that I really <i>want</i> to talk about, but I'll not since they may get cut or simplified since they're... probably Watcher-Ideas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T08:52:26-08:00","id":1000817,"title":"","content":"Sounds good - I'll look for that sometime soon-ish. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T09:49:32-08:00","id":1000849,"title":"","content":"Since you have Buddhist deities like Kannon too, I'm curious do you guys use Siddhartha Gautama around the table, if so how?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11643-corax","name":"Corax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T12:14:11-08:00","id":1000925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000849#post1000849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since you have Buddhist deities like Kannon too, I'm curious do you guys use Siddhartha Gautama around the table, if so how?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh man, yeah, so. This is complicated. Siddharta Gautama has never shown up at our table, but when writing the Kami and looking at the Shen, we spent a lot of time talking about the Buddhists. We think we may one day like to write a PsP, possibly with some alternate powers, for the Buddhist deities like Kannon, and Jizo. This PsP would be shared by all other Buddhist deities across various Pantheons, with possibly some unique powers here and there for different Pantheons.<br /> <br /> The Buddhists occupy a <i>fascinating</i> but utterly tricky spot in Scion. We have not spent a huge amount of time talking about it, but my general idea is they are a Pantheon-Less-Pantheon. I think I will represent Guan Yin, Kannon, Jizo, and their like as Gods and Goddesses who are 'part' of a local Pantheon (such as the Kami or Shen), but don't share that Pantheon's PsP or Virtues. It's... very strange, I realize, but I think it would be a good way to represent the way in which Buddhism interacted, and was incorporated (in Japan, with great resistance by some groups. Amaterasu still bans even mentioning anything that even <i>relates</i> to Buddhism in her grand shrine) into local traditions.<br /> <br /> But, for the Buddhas themselves. We have not spent a large amount of time on this, but we are thinking Titans. They are just so monumentally powerful in-universe that they don't work as Legend 12 Deities (such as when the Buddha intercedes when SunWukong has single handedly fought the Shen), so, Titanhood seems to be a good spot for them. We are not totally sold on it though, it's a bit of a toss up. We want to respect their overwhelming power and understanding, which the best way to do is make them Titans. They have never shown up at our table though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 11-09-2016, 03:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T12:19:33-08:00","id":1000927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000792#post1000792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope, no worried about the Susano-o/Hachiman changes. I figure if the research doesn't support them, then that's fine with me, and if I really need Susano-o to be a Storm God, I can go right ahead and do so in my games, and no-one's going to tell me I can't. Any chance of a rewrite for the Shen? Oh, and I wondered - no Pantheon-specific Purview for the Kami?</div> </div> </div> </div>I hope I'm not revealing too much internal information, but we have begun researching the Shen some time ago - and got stuck for the moment because life caught up with us. No matter when we'll be able to fully confront them, the Shen will take considerable time in any case. There is an abundance of source materials, an exceptionally long history to consider, and a lot of religious debate and contradiction to untangle. What I can say is that they will probably look radically different from the 1e Celestial Bureaucracy, and that I love our ideas for their Titans.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T12:20:31-08:00","id":1000929,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sacerdos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000927#post1000927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I hope I'm not revealing too much internal information, but we have begun researching the Shen some time ago - and got stuck for the moment because life caught up with us. No matter when we'll be able to fully confront them, the Shen will take considerable time in any case. There is an abundance of source materials, an exceptionally long history to consider, and a lot of religious debate and contradiction to untangle. What I can say is that they will probably look radically different from the 1e Celestial Bureaucracy, and that I love our ideas for their Titans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, we totally did already work out some Titans for them, I forgot about that.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T13:42:31-08:00","id":1000963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000925#post1000925\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh man, yeah, so. This is complicated. Siddharta Gautama has never shown up at our table, but when writing the Kami and looking at the Shen, we spent a lot of time talking about the Buddhists. We think we may one day like to write a PsP, possibly with some alternate powers, for the Buddhist deities like Kannon, and Jizo. This PsP would be shared by all other Buddhist deities across various Pantheons, with possibly some unique powers here and there for different Pantheons.<br /> <br /> The Buddhists occupy a <i>fascinating</i> but utterly tricky spot in Scion. We have not spent a huge amount of time talking about it, but my general idea is they are a Pantheon-Less-Pantheon. I think I will represent Guan Yin, Kannon, Jizo, and their like as Gods and Goddesses who are 'part' of a local Pantheon (such as the Kami or Shen), but don't share that Pantheon's PsP or Virtues. It's... very strange, I realize, but I think it would be a good way to represent the way in which Buddhism interacted, and was incorporated (in Japan, with great resistance by some groups. Amaterasu still bans even mentioning anything that even <i>relates</i> to Buddhism in her grand shrine) into local traditions.<br /> <br /> But, for the Buddhas themselves. We have not spent a large amount of time on this, but we are thinking Titans. They are just so monumentally powerful in-universe that they don't work as Legend 12 Deities (such as when the Buddha intercedes when SunWukong has single handedly fought the Shen), so, Titanhood seems to be a good spot for them. We are not totally sold on it though, it's a bit of a toss up. We want to respect their overwhelming power and understanding, which the best way to do is make them Titans. They have never shown up at our table though.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an intriguing way to implement Buddhism as a Pantheon that's syncreticly merged with all the East Asian pantheons. Buddhism is such a tricky mess to fit into Scion, I've read of people using Siddhartha and the other Buddhas as Incarnated Scions of Vishnu but I think it's pretty reductive to just plug in one of the world's most practiced and varied religions into the Deva.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11643-corax","name":"Corax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T13:56:39-08:00","id":1000969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000963#post1000963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's an intriguing way to implement Buddhism as a Pantheon that's syncreticly merged with all the East Asian pantheons. Buddhism is such a tricky mess to fit into Scion, I've read of people using Siddhartha and the other Buddhas as Incarnated Scions of Vishnu but I think it's pretty reductive to just plug in one of the world's most practiced and varied religions into the Deva.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, Buddhism is the big complicated issue for Scion east of the Bosphorus. I think the way we're debating it is the... not the best, but one of the least problematic options out there. Making a unified Buddhist Pantheon doesn't really work since there's a huge amount of difference place to place, but making them actual 'members' of local Pantheons takes away their own nature, and causes issues. Big one with the Kami is that Amaterasu doesn't like a system that messes with Imperial Authority, and Buddhism does just that. So, Kannon, and Jizo are presented here as they are <i>incredibly</i> important, but they are not necessarily members of the Pantheon. I would say probably different Virtues, and for sure a different PsP.<br /> <br /> Just making them part of the Deva also creates a... lot of issues, lots of internal religious strife there between Buddhism and Hinduism. So, I'd personally be strongly against just making the Buddhists a sub-sect of the Deva for many, many reasons. I understand why people may do it, it is the simplest answer, and if that works for your games, go for it. I'm just a stupid academic who worries about the legitimacy of me trying to address cultures I have no connection to (which is all of them, the past is a different country, cultural relativism, all that) so I try to go to the farthest yard just to combat issues I wrestle with.<br /> <br /> That's actually one of the reasons the Kami is fifty pages, mostly single spaced, and font size ten. It is possibly the longest thing I have ever written, and it's that long since I desperately tried to understand the context so I could try to make as few mistakes as I possibly could.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T20:50:41-08:00","id":1001175,"title":"","content":"So, on a scale of 1 to 10, how bad of an idea would trying to seperate the Shen by dynasty be?<br /> <br /> Because I know that they're kind of just a bunch of different mythologies that all got thrown together, so seperating them wouldn't exactly be innaccurate, but I also know that it would take so much effort that it's ridiculous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12300,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","user":"Crying","date":". 11-10-2016, 02:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T22:15:55-08:00","id":1001197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001175#post1001175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, on a scale of 1 to 10, how bad of an idea would trying to seperate the Shen by dynasty be?<br /> <br /> Because I know that they're kind of just a bunch of different mythologies that all got thrown together, so seperating them wouldn't exactly be accurate, but I also know that it would take so much effort that it's ridiculous.</div> </div> </div> </div>How good of an idea would it be? I can see why someone may want to try it, but it would become an absolute mess. The Shen are a large collection of different mythological traditions that are all combined together, just like you said. But, under the influence of the State, though just which State it technically is may change, these traditions are... I would not quite say harmonised, but unified to one degree or another. I think the Shen are best represented as the unified conglomerate, or Divine 'State', it really encapsulates a lot of the feel, and historical themes from what I have read of them thus far.<br /> <br /> The biggest difficulty in trying to divide the Shen by chronology would be a massive number of cross-over Deities. Especially by the later, it would just be compounding upon itself, with more and more. I think it would be an undertaking in the realm of the possible, but, not in any sensible time frame. And then you would have the mess of each Pantheon probably having a wildly different PsP in some cases, it would take years. Or, well, it would take us years.<br /> <br /> I find the Shen exceptionally interesting due to their shifting traditions. In the Shen, we see a huge slosh of ideas, Gods, Goddesses, mortals, politics, all influencing each other in a frankly big mess. But, I do <i>love</i> messes like that. I look forward to the day myself and Sac sit down together and resume our work on the Shen, I think they will be very interesting in the end.<br /> <br /> Honestly, biggest thing with the Shen that will prove tricky is the habit of mortals to become Deities. With the Kami, it can be explained as humanity being the descendants of various Kami. (We actually have a little idea for how Kami Scions come about based around that, it will likely be in a side bar when we put the information up on our website.) But the Shen just have mortals become Deities, and that is a lot of fun to play with in multi-Pantheon games.<br /> <br /> The Theoi, Anunna, and Bogovi have their little dirt-dumb-barely-even-people mortals, while the Shen have mortals who occasionally become Major Deities. And then the Irish have mortals who not only waged war against the Gods, but won, and killed most of them. Mortals are great fun in cross-Pantheon games when you have such different variations in just what it means to be a mortal. It has never come up in my games yet, but one day I might have the differences between mortals come up. Or I might possibly bind it to geography as I have done other things. So, either a descendent from Mil is potentially dangerous to beings with Legend, or just when you are in Ireland, the line between Mortal and Divine is a bit blurry.<br /> <br /> Bit rambling there, sorry about that. It's late over here.<br /> <br /> In short. It is possible to divide up the Shen by dynasty, but in the same way that climbing Everest is possible, and I think it would lose a lot of the uniqueness of the Pantheon in doing so. Further, you would have compounding issues in later dynasties, and in earlier ones, they exist in a Mythic-History where we don't have the best grasp of what was going on Pantheon-wise.<br /> <br /> Edit: So that number out of ten. I'd give it a 9/10. Not necessarily the worst idea ever of all time, but in doing so, the Pantheons would be a mess, and something of their primary nature would be lost.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T23:26:30-08:00","id":1001216,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001197#post1001197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So that number out of ten. I'd give it a 9/10. Not necessarily the worst idea ever of all time, but in doing so, the Pantheons would be a mess, and something of their primary nature would be lost.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I thought it would be something like that...<br /> <br /> It would still be cool if there was some way to represent the whole 'not all the Shen are the same, they're a bunch of different religious ideas and pantheons thrown together' thing though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T23:42:25-08:00","id":1001222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001175#post1001175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, on a scale of 1 to 10, how bad of an idea would trying to seperate the Shen by dynasty be?<br /> <br /> Because I know that they're kind of just a bunch of different mythologies that all got thrown together, so seperating them wouldn't exactly be accurate, but I also know that it would take so much effort that it's ridiculous.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I think you're imagining the changes in Chinese religion over time as a bit <i>too</i> monumental. Deities from pre-imperial times are still being worshipped - it's not like each dynasty would bring a completely new set of family deities or anything. Quite the contrary, actually. The imperial Chinese ruling class, in fact, tried to distance itself from religion as much as possible, at least in theory. Confucianism, the traditional religion of elites, was largely agnostic, and Buddhist, Daoist, and folk deities were often derided as superstitious nonsense by the ruling class. Thus, records of the <i>actual</i> religious practices of the Chinese people are sketchy for a long time: It isn't until the ruling class opens up to folk religion, and the invention of printing and wider literacy increase the records that we can really grasp the broadth of the Chinese pantheon. That would be the Song Dynasty, with the relevant period starting around the 11th century. That's like the last quarter of Chinese history. So from that point on, those dynasty pantheons you're suggesting would be pretty similar, and before that, they would be pretty hard to do at all. It would be possible to do an &quot;Ancient Chinese&quot; pantheon with just figures like N\u00fcwa and Huang Di, but it's not like those figures became completely irrelevant later, and frankly, I don't see the fun in half a pantheon.<br /> <br /> No, I don't think division by dynasty would work, but you could, in theory, divide the pantheon by creed. Some nuance would be lost, as there is a lot of syncretism and cross-over between those beliefs, but it's not as bad as in Japan (we don't properly explain that in the write-up for the Kami as it is now, because it will be a sidebar, but Buddhism and Shinto were historically one religion, the split there is fairly recent; in China, however, there has always been an awareness, at least among intellectuals, that there were several religions to choose from - although the common people still mixed them). I did consider doing that for some time when I began research, but I found that it would represent Chinese society badly, and would lose a lot of fun. But in theory, it's possible and makes a certain amount of sense. The five major sources for Chinese religion and mythology I'd name are ancient traditions, Confucianism, Daoism, Buddhism, and folk religion. An ancient pantheon would include members like N\u00fcwa, Huang Di, and possibly Xi Wang Mu, though I can't really make a guess for a PSP for that one. A Confucian pantheon is a bad idea, since, as I mentioned, Confucianism is pretty much agnostic-atheistic, and depicting Confucius and his students as deities would probably be considered quite disrespectful. A Daoist pantheon would be hard to do just for the enormous amount of highly complicated sources that religion produced, but it would then include figures like the Jade Emperor, the Three Pure Ones, the Azure Lad of Heaven, and could also contain Xi Wang Mu; the Daoist PSP would be about everything being unified in the flow of the Dao, about natural chaos triumphing over order, and about achieving absolute immortality (when we last talked about it, Daoism was probable as the main source for our Shen PSP, but nothing is certain at the moment). A Chinese Buddhist pantheon would be a bit of a strange choice (Watcher already mentioned how it's hard to separate Buddhist deities from the cultures they were adapted to), but it could still be centred around what makes these adaptations of the Buddhist deties uniquely Chinese, such as Guanyin's femininity; its PSP would be the usual Buddhist things, rebirth, incarnation, enlightenment, overcoming illusion, all that. A folk pantheon would again be a bit hard to separate from the Daoists (especially because it would need the Jade Emperor to function), but it would certainly be the most fun, with hordes of deified mortals acting as colourful, very fallible gods like Guan Yu, Mazu, Wenchang, and Sun Wukong; again, I would be a bit hard-pressed for a unique PSP to give that one. So, as I said, dividing the Chinese pantheon wouldn't be my choice, but it would be possible, and I would encourage you to do it, just because I'd be interested in seeing <i>how </i>you do it. But we'll do it differently. That'll mean about 15 major gods, and a literal armada of minor gods, not to mention months of reading and research, and probably even more typing than for the Kami. It'll be a hell of a lot of work and it'll take forever, but I'm pretty sure it will be grand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T14:57:49-08:00","id":1001939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1000925#post1000925\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But, for the Buddhas themselves. We have not spent a large amount of time on this, but we are thinking Titans. They are just so monumentally powerful in-universe that they don't work as Legend 12 Deities (such as when the Buddha intercedes when SunWukong has single handedly fought the Shen), so, Titanhood seems to be a good spot for them. We are not totally sold on it though, it's a bit of a toss up. We want to respect their overwhelming power and understanding, which the best way to do is make them Titans. They have never shown up at our table though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gautama Buddha, former Scion of Vishnu and current Avatar of Nirvana, Titanrealm of Nothingness?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T16:32:43-08:00","id":1001974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001939#post1001939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Gautama Buddha, former Scion of Vishnu and current Avatar of Nirvana, Titanrealm of Nothingness?</div> </div> </div> </div>I probably wouldn't ever explain any parentage or anything. Actually, well, no. I treat every religious system as 100% true internally, even when this makes zero sense or is contradictory to other stated things (in which case I have two things that are both 100% true even if they disagree). So, Gautama Buddha would be a Prince, nothing more. I would not say he was a Scion, I would simply say that he is exactly who is is internally.<br /> <br /> Now, externally, other Pantheons may argue this. The Deva may, or, well, actually. Sac would know this better, but I think the Kami may even claim him, since I believe there was a very odd movement at one point tying into the Japanese Exceptionalism where they claimed to be the source of Buddhism. But, I would treat this as nothing but rhetoric, with The Truth being the Buddhist conception of things.<br /> <br /> But, what Titanrealm he would be bound to, I have no idea. I have been moving away from the ideas of Titanrealms in my revisions really. I don't like the idea specific Titanrealms are picking on specific Pantheons, I run it more insular. Balor (War Titan), Bres (Justice Titan), and Cethlenn (Prophecy Titan) would wage war to try to conquer or subjugate Ireland. Balor would be rather uninterested in the Shen however, and wouldn't get involved unless dragged in by politics. So, if I wrote up the Buddhists, I might not have the Buddhas bound to specific Titanrealms. So far, I have only been noting the Major Power each Titan is linked to by making the Power in Bold. (For example, Atlas has Epic Strength in bold, and Izanami has Death in bold)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T16:58:12-08:00","id":1001985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001974#post1001974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I probably wouldn't ever explain any parentage or anything. Actually, well, no. I treat every religious system as 100% true internally, even when this makes zero sense or is contradictory to other stated things (in which case I have two things that are both 100% true even if they disagree). So, Gautama Buddha would be a Prince, nothing more. I would not say he was a Scion, I would simply say that he is exactly who is is internally.<br /> <br /> Now, externally, other Pantheons may argue this. The Deva may, or, well, actually. Sac would know this better, but I think the Kami may even claim him, since I believe there was a very odd movement at one point tying into the Japanese Exceptionalism where they claimed to be the source of Buddhism. But, I would treat this as nothing but rhetoric, with The Truth being the Buddhist conception of things.</div> </div> </div> </div><i>Shrug </i>Your choice man, the idea just struck me and I thought it was worth suggesting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But, what Titanrealm he would be bound to, I have no idea. I have been moving away from the ideas of Titanrealms in my revisions really. I don't like the idea specific Titanrealms are picking on specific Pantheons, I run it more insular. Balor (War Titan), Bres (Justice Titan), and Cethlenn (Prophecy Titan) would wage war to try to conquer or subjugate Ireland. Balor would be rather uninterested in the Shen however, and wouldn't get involved unless dragged in by politics. So, if I wrote up the Buddhists, I might not have the Buddhas bound to specific Titanrealms. So far, I have only been noting the Major Power each Titan is linked to by making the Power in Bold. (For example, Atlas has Epic Strength in bold, and Izanami has Death in bold) </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I saw that actually. I'm rather fond of the Titanrealms, but that's not a bad way of doing things. I even contemplated it myself at one point, but I just like the Titanrealms too much to get rid of them. At most I'd just get rid of the idea of a dominant Avatar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T17:57:48-08:00","id":1002015,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah, no worries! I'm just musing on how I would manage all of it myself. I totally get tracking paths of Divinity, moving from Pantheon to Pantheon with origins of ideas, having an order to the divergent systems. I hold nothing against people who do it otherwise. The revisions I do tend to be reflections of how I run and order cosmologies, which is why I shape them around my 'rules' of existence. I welcome people to do otherwise, use stuff I have written, and reinterpret it. I don't mind at all.<br /> <br /> The Titanrealms are a really nice system, they give locations for God level Stories, and really help provide structure to a part of the game that is exceptionally structureless otherwise (God Tier). I am, however, an absolute lunatic. The biggest killing point of it for me was some Titans (Atlas, Izanami, Typhon, Raijin + Fujin) being bound (or 'bound' in the case of the Storm Oni) in specific, non-Titanrealm locals. So, I have ended up breaking the Titanrealms down, and having each Titan bound (or 'bound', I like a lot of figurative Bindings. Lir bound by his sorrow, Raijin by conversion, Kagutsuchi being broken into different Kami) specifically where they are noted to be.<br /> <br /> This does create a bunch of difficulties really, and I would say it is a <i>lot</i> of trouble, and effort. I totally acknowledge that this really is just me being a glutton for punishment, and giving myself a huge amount of work to explain things such as &quot;Why has no one seen Atlas when he's supposed to be just right over there&quot; or, &quot;Why have Scientists not noticed Typhon sealed under a mountain.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-12T00:28:39-08:00","id":1002144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1001939#post1001939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Gautama Buddha, former Scion of Vishnu and current Avatar of Nirvana, Titanrealm of Nothingness?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the really tricky thing here is, as Watcher alludes to, that in Buddhist cosmology, the Buddha known as Siddharta Gautama (in the following I'll refer to him as Shakyamuni, another one of his titles) is neither the first nor the last Buddha. Depending on the source, there's a whole of seven, 21, or even hundreds of Buddhas. &quot;Buddha&quot; is not a person, but a stage and class of being. In every age (and these ages each are millennia long), a Buddha descends from heaven in human form to teach the ignorant people of that age the Law of Buddhism; after his death, people first practice his teaching, then start to forget it, then descend into ignorance again (according to most authorities, we've been in this stage for some centuries now) until they have fully forgotten the Law. Thus, sooner or later, a new Buddha will have to descend and start over.<br /> <br /> Among the most important of the Buddhas beside Shakyamuni are Vairochana, the esoteric &quot;Sun Buddha&quot; of the very first age, supposed to have existed before the universe and being the source and underlying true nature of everything that exists (under the name &quot;Dainichi&quot; he's very important to Japanese religion; that's one of the points where, as Watcher pointed out, the medieval Japanese tried to find the origin of Buddhism in their own country: They associated Dainichi with Amaterasu and thus claimed that Dainichi, the cosmos, and Buddhist Law had all been born on Japanese soil); Amitabha, the &quot;Merciful Buddha&quot;, who made 21 oaths to aid in the enlightenment of all sentient beings, thus creating the Pure Land souls can be reborn into to easily achieve enlightenment there (Avalokiteshvara, as the being you might know from Japan, China and Tibet respectively as Kannon, Guanyin and Chenrezig, is the main agent of Amitabha); and Maitreya, the &quot;Buddha of the Future&quot; who will enlighten the next age, but who can aid us now already as a Bodhisattva.<br /> <br /> Altogether, the Buddhas are all extremely wise and powerful, in fact literally more powerful than gods (who are, in Buddhist cosmology, actually among the lowest classes of heavenly beings), yet they are detached, they act in the world only through intermediaries (the Bodhisattvas, mainly, enlightened beings who have chosen to help other beings, and who won't achieve Nirvana themselves before all other beings are enlightened as well), because part of enlightenment is cutting all ties with the material world. Thus, they are in a way absolutely exemplary Titans or Primordials (at least when one ignores the antagonism suggested by 1e). Assigning them to Titanrealms, however, is more problematic. First, characterising Nirvana only as &quot;Nothingness&quot; falls a bit flat of the complicated philosophical history of that concept. After all, if achieving Nirvana simply meant disappearing into nothingness, it would make no sense for Buddhas to still appear and act in myths all over Asia, as they certainly do. What exactly the Nirvana is has been discussed for millennia now, and I'm certainly not fit to depict that discussion. In any case, however, you could probably procure enough Buddhas to fill a whole Titanrealm with them alone.<br /> <br /> In fact, a Buddhist pantheon would be totally feasible, with the Buddhas as a group of benevolent Titans at the top, and figures like Bodhisattvas, Arhats and Wisdom Kings in place of the usual gods (as I mentioned, &quot;gods&quot; (Devas) as such are too low in Buddhist cosmology to usually figure into such a depiction); they have cosmology, antagonists (Mara) and everything. We have, however, decided against doing something like that (at least for now), because it would, on the one hand, erase the local uniqueness the Buddhist pantheon itself took on from culture to culture (like how Avalokiteshvara is gender-ambigious in Japan, female in China, and male in Tibet), and, by removing them from the individual Asian pantheons, create a lot of loose ends within them - without Jigoku, Japan would have no functional underworld, the Seven Lucky Gods without their Buddhicised members would just be Ebisu talking to six imaginary friends, the whole culture and religion of the country would be impossible to explain without its Buddhist foundations. So I'm always very much for including Buddhist elements in the depictions of Asian pantheons - though you could <i>still</i> do an &quot;original&quot; Buddhist pantheon based on Indian scripture with Shakyamuni, Vairochana, Avalokiteshvara, Kshitigarbha, and so on in addition to the other Asian ones. I can't say whether Scion 2.0 will ever do this, as with the other Asian pantheons, Buddhism isn't exactly underrepresented, and there are still lots of other regions of the world to do, but even if, it's probably pretty far away.<br /> <br /> To come back to your original suggestion at last, treating Shakyamuni as a Scion of Vishnu would of course bring certain cotradictions with it, because Vishnu is basically non-existent within Buddhist religion itself. The relationships between Hinduism and Buddhism as a whole are historically quite complicated. One important fact is that what we would call Hinduism today is in fact ca. 1,500 years younger than Buddhism - although pinpointing that exactly is hard, because Vedic religion is root to both traditions, and that again is at least 700 years older than Buddhism. In any case, Hinduism for a long time saw Buddhism as a heretic off-shoot of their own religion; thus, Hindu clerics claimed that the Buddha Shakyamuni was in fact the ninth Avatar of Vishnu, incarnated to delude all the weak-minded with false teachings and thus rightfully damn them. This was integrated into the myths about the gods' strategies against demons: The reason demons could so often become dangerous opponents to the gods was that they would accumulate supernatural power through piety and religious rites - by teaching them religion and rites that were essentially wrong and worthless, Vishnu made the demons powerless. As you can easily see, that's a very unflattering way of depicting another religion, and thus we don't really want to have that influence our depiction of Buddhism, but to be fair, opinion became more positive over time, although not unanimously so. Buddhism was then sometimes seen as something of a worthy alternative, a brother even, to Hinduism, and Vishnu's incarnation as Shakyamuni came to be seen more as a gift to world (this coincides with Buddhism beginning to influence Hindu teaching much more strongly; the hell of Naraka, for example, has little foundation in older Hindu scripture and is pretty much an adaptation of a purely Buddhist idea). Thus, there are some Hindus today who'd say that other religious founders like Jesus and Mohammed were avatars of Vishnu as well (Vishnu, after all, has thousands of avatars; the ten everyone knows are just his purest incarnations). You can do with that info what you like, but for this project, we prefer to stick to what a religion says about itself mainly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-29T07:41:25-08:00","id":1009783,"title":"","content":"As a big fan of the Kami, I just wanted to thank you for all the hard work you put into this. It's a great read and I could already see potential characters forming in my head just reading through your God list. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3714-mysteris","name":"Mysteris","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"87"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-29T13:03:05-08:00","id":1009908,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mysteris</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1009783#post1009783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a big fan of the Kami, I just wanted to thank you for all the hard work you put into this. It's a great read and I could already see potential characters forming in my head just reading through your God list. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Glad you enjoyed it! Just knowing that someone's enjoyed reading it makes writing it totally worth it. If you've got any questions on it, or want any extra information, just let us know.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-29T15:32:34-08:00","id":1009969,"title":"","content":"I also found your remake quite interesting, Watcher! I never knew that Susano-o was not a Storm God and that Hachiman actually isn't much of a fighter, and all those insights into the intricacies of Japanese culture during the Kami's heyday was just fascinating to read.<br /> <br /> \u200bKeep up the good work! Any ideas which Pantheon you're thinking about working on next? If you're even planning to do any next?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T01:02:49-08:00","id":1010100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>unnatural1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1009969#post1009969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also found your remake quite interesting, Watcher! I never knew that Susano-o was not a Storm God and that Hachiman actually isn't much of a fighter, and all those insights into the intricacies of Japanese culture during the Kami's heyday was just fascinating to read.<br /> <br /> \u200bKeep up the good work! Any ideas which Pantheon you're thinking about working on next? If you're even planning to do any next?</div> </div> </div> </div>Susanoo's storm associations are a difficult topic, and I just wrote a beautiful, long explanation about that, but then I accidentally refreshed the page (because when I'm not explaining mythology, I'm actually an idiot), so you get the trimmed down version. There'll be a sidebar as well when this goes up on the site proper. Basically, it is a very, very old scholarly reconstruction that is, however, still often repeated even in academic works. The assumption is based on a number of points: To form a proper triad with Amaterasu (sun) and Tsukuyomi (moon), the scholar supposed Susanoo had to represent a heavenly element as well - combined with the lordship over the sea, that apparently looked like storms to our scholar (the sea actually ties in far better with his afterlife associations, as e.g. Tokoyo should prove); also, his bad temper and dragon-slaying seem quite typical of a storm god. The obvious problem with this nice idea is that Susanoo never actually does thunder-wind-and-lightening-stuff, nor is he called a storm god in texts, nor worshipped as such. And he is worshipped as a great many things, being one of the most flexible Kami association-wise: He is a culture hero and divine smith in Izumo, a pestilence and death god at Gion Shrine in Kyoto, a mountain god at Kumano Shrine, a sword and war spirit at Atsuta Shrine, and so on.<br /> <br /> This points to one of the main things to realise about the Kami: In spite of Japanese religion having some animist elements, it's mostly no use looking for big elemental associations for each god - their role as family ancestors and patrons is much more important. Many Kami, even important ones, actually have no &quot;powers&quot; at all, simply being the patron of family XYZ. And then, sometimes, they just pick up stuff associated with that family. Hachiman is actually the best example of that: He was originally a deity kinda artificially added to the Imperial family pantheon for political reasons, no war associations whatsoever. But due to lineage and inheritance, he became the family deity of the Minamoto clan, who in turn became the most succesful warriors of their time. Thus, Hachiman came to be seen as a granter of victory and protector of samurai (although still a peaceful Buddhist deity himself and not a war god like e.g. Ares).<br /> <br /> And that, I personally think, is the key to understanding Susanoo as well. I'd see him, originally, not as the god of anything in particular, but as the patron of the ruling families of the Izumo region. We have to remember that, before being unified in remote antiquity by the conquests of the kingdom of Yamato, Japan consisted of many separate kingdoms with separate traditions. Izumo was amongst the last to be brought into the fold, and their power is attested to by Susanoo, who probably was their original patron, joining the imperial pantheon and his adventures being given disproportionate attention in the imperial myth cycle. Thus, he picked up associations from what people thought of Izumo over time: First he stood for the cultural innovations brought to Japan from Korea via that Western province, then for chaos and rebellion as Izumo stood against the Yamato state, then for the death and magic Izumo came to be known for in popular superstition, and so on.<br /> <br /> Regarding future projects, I can, as already mentioned in previous posts, make no promises. Watcher is working on a Wyandot pantheon, and we both started looking into the Shen, although both of those projects have fallen victim to us being busy for now. I'm also accumulating info on the Egyptian, Indian, Yoruba, Polynesian, and Ancient Near Eastern religions, so we'll see when I manage to push those <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> So for the moment, for you as much as for us, it's wait and see.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T19:50:13-08:00","id":1010464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>unnatural1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/999859-the-kami-revision?p=1009969#post1009969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also found your remake quite interesting, Watcher! I never knew that Susano-o was not a Storm God and that Hachiman actually isn't much of a fighter, and all those insights into the intricacies of Japanese culture during the Kami's heyday was just fascinating to read.<br /> <br /> \u200bKeep up the good work! Any ideas which Pantheon you're thinking about working on next? If you're even planning to do any next?</div> </div> </div> </div>Susano-O and Hachiman are really interesting for those reasons. Susano-O is a great example of when a small misconception has huge ramifications. One Victorian (or Edwardian? Can't remember) academic misunderstood a text, made a judgement call, and resulted in a misconception growing colossal in size. Susano-O as a Storm God is somewhat an entrenched idea in Western perceptions, all because of one slight mistake more than a century ago.<br /> <br /> Hachiman is also a ton of fun since he's a God of Warriors, not a Warrior God, or God of War. That slight variation in wording causes such a huge shift. Hachiman is one of the more interesting Divine Parent options in my mind because of this, rather unique among the Deities with the War Purview I have encountered so far.<br /> <br /> And, as for what's next, Sacerdos hit the nail on the head pretty much. I've got one essay left this semester, and I need to send in an application to graduate school. The moment winter break hits, I'll probably decide what's next on my plate. I have some tentative work done on the Wyandot (named the Huron by the Europeans), reading several collections of myth, and folklore from them. I am slightly reticent to try to write them up however, I am not entirely sure I could do them justice with the resources I have at the moment. I would be concerned that I would misrepresent them somehow due to the sources I have.<br /> <br /> The Shen are interesting, and they might get a focus next for revisions. I am at a slight crossroads at the moment however. With Scion 2e coming out, I am torn between waiting to work on some fan stuff for that, or keep working on 1e. I research and write these to relax however, so I will probably work on <i>something</i> over the winter break. A small part of me wants to do the Welsh, but the more I look at them, the more I see a gnarled mess which I don't think I can pierce without a very concerted effort.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}